== ARCHIVE: benzene-monomer CCSD(T)/6-31g* timing anchor ==

Why this exists. Two claims needed a measured middle point, and both would otherwise have
been arithmetic I made up:

  1. How long the queued benzene jobs (tcc job 93285, 苯-水 / 苯-氨 / 苯-甲烷, 114-118 AO)
     will take. The archived ladder only spans the water dimer at 36-82 AO, and its (T)
     seconds are printed to one decimal (0.1, 0.9), which cannot anchor an extrapolation.
  2. Whether the benzene dimer (192 AO) is out of reach because of memory.

Measured here, on this box, 8 threads, through the production function
`nlcsplit/step_m_ccsdt.correlated()` - same call path and same frozen-core convention as the
ladder, so the seconds are comparable rather than merely similar.

    苯单体  nao=96  nbas=48  nelec=42   occ=21  vir=75
      frozen(cores), 6 pairs frozen (相关 occ=15)   rung 13.1 s   CCSD 7.2 s  (T) 5.1 s
      all-electron,  0 pairs frozen (相关 occ=21)   rung 26.7 s   CCSD 13.8 s (T) 12.4 s
      E_ref(RHF) = -230.7019770331 Ha in both rungs, as it must be.

Two things this settles and one it does not.

It settles that a 96-AO closed-shell CCSD(T) in this code path costs tens of seconds, not
hours - which is why the earlier guess of "3-10 hours per benzene complex" was wrong and why
the queued job is expected inside an hour once it starts.

It does NOT settle the benzene-dimer question, and this archive is where that is recorded.
The bottom block of the output computes C(occ,3)*vir^3 doubles, giving ~102 GB for the dimer
at correlated occ=30, vir=150. That figure is not a memory requirement: `pyscf/cc/ccsd_t.py`
line 101 sizes the (T) contraction buffer as
`(max_memory*0.5e6/8 - nocc**3*3*num_threads()) / (nocc*nmo)` and the triple-indexed work is
block-contracted over virtual pairs (`prange_tril`), so the full array is never materialised.
Quoting 102 GB (or the 729 GB you get from nocc^3*nvir^3 instead of the combination count) as
"why the dimer is not computed" would be an unverified claim about another program's memory
behaviour, and paper/main.md §3.2 therefore still says what was measured, not this.

To appear: reproduce with
    PYTHONPATH=. OMP_NUM_THREADS=8 python3 -u scratch/tune_benzene_mono.py
The benzene geometry is the first fragment of the official S22 entry 11 (geomlib.S22_system),
so the monomer leg matches the dimer rungs already archived in this directory.

